Process memory layout



Text segment: machine instructions; shareable between identical processes; read-only
Data segment: for initialized data; e.g., int count = 99;
BSS (block started by symbol) segment: uninitialized data; e.g., int sum[10];
Heap: dynamic memory allocation
Stack: initial arguments and environment; stack frames

Pipe bugs
int pipefd[2];
pipe (pipefd);

if (fork () == 0)
  exit (0);

char buffer[10];
read (pipefd[0], buffer, sizeof (buffer));

What will happen here?

Parent will block indefinitely. Since the child exits without writing anything, and the parent has not closed its own write end, the read end never sees EOF (EOF is only signaled when all write ends are closed).

Creating and joining
#include &lt;pthread.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

void *worker(void *arg) {
    int id = *(int *)arg;
    printf("hello from thread %d\n", id);
    return NULL;
}

int main(void) {
    pthread_t t1, t2;
    int a = 1, b = 2;
    pthread_create(&t1, NULL, worker, &a);
    pthread_create(&t2, NULL, worker, &b);
    pthread_join(t1, NULL);
    pthread_join(t2, NULL);
    return 0;
}

Walk through with the highlight steps:

worker function - every pthread entry point has this signature: takes a void *, returns a void *. That’s how you pass arguments in and get results out. We cast to int * to recover the argument.
thread handles and args - pthread_t is an opaque handle - think of it like a PID but for threads. Note we pass pointers to a and b. A common bug: passing &i from a loop counter - by the time the thread runs, i has moved on.
create - this is where a new kernel thread is actually spawned. After this call returns, two threads are running. No guarantee which one runs first, or even whether the parent reaches the next line before the child finishes.
join - the parent blocks until each child finishes. Without join, main could return and the whole process exits while workers are mid-printf. join is also how you collect the return value - we’re passing NULL because we don’t care here.

Mutex example
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
long counter = 0;

void *bump(void *arg) {
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 1000000; i++) {
        pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
        counter++;              // critical section
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
    }
    return NULL;
}

Now the increment is safe - but threads may spend time waiting
Keep critical sections small: lock, touch shared data, unlock


Walk through the highlights:

globals - PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER is a compile-time initializer for statically allocated mutexes. For dynamic allocation you’d use pthread_mutex_init. The mutex has to be shared - if each thread had its own mutex, it would defend nothing.
lock - this line is the gate. If another thread is already inside, we block here. The kernel can put us to sleep; we don’t spin forever.
critical section - this is now atomic with respect to other threads that use the same lock. Note: only threads that also lock &lock respect this. A rogue thread doing counter++ without the lock still breaks everything. Locks are a convention.
unlock - critical: don’t forget this. If you return or exit early inside the critical section without unlocking, every other thread blocks forever. This is a big source of bugs in C; RAII in C++ and defer in Go exist specifically to prevent it.

Tradeoff to flag: we’ve serialized part of the work. If the critical section is huge, we’ve basically made the program single-threaded. Lock small regions.

Producer/consumer sketch
pthread_mutex_t m = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
pthread_cond_t  not_empty = PTHREAD_COND_INITIALIZER;
queue_t q;

void *consumer(void *_) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&m);
    while (queue_empty(&q))
        pthread_cond_wait(&not_empty, &m);
    item_t x = queue_pop(&q);
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&m);
    process(x);
    return NULL;
}

void *producer(void *_) {
    item_t x = make_item();
    pthread_mutex_lock(&m);
    queue_push(&q, x);
    pthread_cond_signal(&not_empty);
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&m);
    return NULL;
}

Walk through the highlights:

shared state - one mutex, one condvar, one queue. The condvar “belongs to” the mutex in the sense that every wait/signal on it happens while holding m.
consumer lock - before we can even check the queue, we need the lock. We can’t safely call queue_empty while a producer might be modifying the queue.
while + cond_wait - classic pattern. Check the condition; if not satisfied, wait. When wait returns, we hold the lock again and the condition might be true - loop back and re-check.
pop and unlock - we know the queue isn’t empty because we just exited the while loop holding the lock. Pop, then release the lock before doing process(x) - no reason to hold the lock during the actual work.
producer lock and push - mirror image: lock, mutate the shared queue.
signal - notify one waiter. broadcast would wake all waiters; use it when the state change might satisfy multiple of them.
unlock - note: we signal then unlock. Signaling while holding the lock is fine and sometimes clearer; the waiter can’t actually proceed until we release anyway.

This pattern - mutex + condvar + while-loop check - is the foundation for essentially all higher-level concurrency primitives: bounded queues, semaphores, barriers, read-write locks. If you internalize this, the rest follows.

Beware naïve implementations

Why not use a simple bool?
bool lock = FALSE
do {
  while (lock == TRUE);
  lock = TRUE;
  //CRITICAL SECTION
  lock = FALSE;
}while(TRUE);
This implementation requires TWO operations: a read (test) and a write (set) that must be atomic

Peterson’s solution

Two processes: \(P_i\) and \(P_j\)
Two shared data items: int turn; bool flag[2];
Pis code:
do {
  flag[i] = TRUE;                // I want to enter critical section
  turn = j;                      // let Pj go first if it wants
  while (flag[j] && turn == j);  // busy wait
  //CRITICAL SECTION
  flag[i] = FALSE;               // Im leaving critical section
  //REMAINDER
}while(TRUE);

turn indicates whose turn it is: turn == i → \(P_i\)’s turn
if flag[i] == true → \(P_i\) is ready for the critical section

To enter: \(P_i\) sets flag[i] = true and turn = j

i.e., \(P_i\) demurs to \(P_j\); if they are both ready turn will get set to?

Whomever runs last sets the turn to the other process

Peterson’s solution (cont’d)
Pis code:
do {
  flag[i] = TRUE;                // I want to enter critical section
  turn = j;                      // let Pj go first if it wants
  while (flag[j] && turn == j);  // busy wait
  //CRITICAL SECTION
  flag[i] = FALSE;               // Im leaving critical section
  //REMAINDER
}while(TRUE);

Does this meet the three solution requirements?
Mutual exclusion: under what conditions can a process be in the critical section?

\(P_i\) only enters if flag[j] is false or turn == i
Conversely, if \(P_i\) and \(P_j\) are in the CS, then flag[i] == flag[j]; this implies both \(P_i\) and \(P_j\) terminated their whiles at the same time; BUT, in order for this to be true, turn must be both 0 and 1 (Contradiction)

Peterson’s solution (cont’d)
Pis code:
do {
  flag[i] = TRUE;                // I want to enter critical section
  turn = j;                      // let Pj go first if it wants
  while (flag[j] && turn == j);  // busy wait
  //CRITICAL SECTION
  flag[i] = FALSE;               // Im leaving critical section
  //REMAINDER
}while(TRUE);

Progress: Is there a condition where \(P_i\) can’t enter the CS?

There is no condition under which \(P_i\) cannot (eventually) enter the CS; either the other process will set turn == i or it is already set

Bounded Waiting: How long will \(P_i\) wait in the best/worst case?

\(P_i\) will enter after at most one entry by \(P_j\)

Peterson’s solution (cont’d)
Pis code:
do {
  flag[i] = TRUE;                // I want to enter critical section
  turn = j;                      // let Pj go first if it wants
  while (flag[j] && turn == j);  // busy wait
  //CRITICAL SECTION
  flag[i] = FALSE;               // Im leaving critical section
  //REMAINDER
}while(TRUE);

Do you see any issues with this solution?

Limitation: Only works for two processes
Modern architectures may also break this solution

Processors and/or compilers can reorder reads and writes that have no dependencies
Multithreaded applications could lead to issues

Does this solution meet the requirements?
bool test_and_set(bool *lock){
  bool ret = *lock;
  *lock = TRUE;
  return ret;
}

lock = FALSE;
do{
  while(test_and_set(&lock));
  // CRITICAL SECTION
  lock = FALSE;
}while(true)

Mutual exclusion?

Yes

Progress?

Yes: when the lock is released, a waiting process will acquire it

Bounded waiting?

No: an unlucky process could be endlessly passed over based on the scheduler’s decisions

Mutex locks (cont’d)

Protect a critical section by

First acquire() a lock
Then release() the lock

What’s going on under the hood?
void lock(lock_t *lock) {
  while (test_and_set(&lock-&gt;flag, 1) == 1)
  ; // spin-wait (do nothing)
}

test_and_set() or compare_and_swap()

Semaphores



Synchronization tool that provides more sophisticated ways (than mutex locks) for processes to synchronize their activities
Semaphore S: integer variable
Can only be accessed via two atomic operations

wait() and signal()

Originally called P() and V() (because, Dutch - another Dijkstra invention)


Do not have ownership, any thread can signal (increment) or wait (decrement)




wait(S) {
    while (S &lt;= 0)
       ; // busy
    S--;
}

signal(S) {
    S++;
}

Semaphores (cont’d)



Binary semaphore: integer value can range only between 0 and 1

Functionally similar to a mutex, but without ownership — any thread can signal it (often when people call something a mutex it’s actually a binary semaphore)





do{
  wait(mutex);
  //CS
  signal(mutex);
}while(TRUE);


Counting semaphore: integer value can range over an unrestricted domain

When would this make sense?

Protect a finite set of resources rather than a single one (e.g., you have a pool of db connections that can be shared by processes)
Initialize S to the number of resources
Decrement S each time someone grabs a resource, increment when they release

Semaphores (cont’d)



Can also be used to force synchronization
Consider \(P_1\) and \(P_2\) that with two statements \(S_1\) and \(S_2\) and the requirement that \(S_1\) to happen before \(S_2\)




P1:
   S1;
   signal(sync);
P2:
   wait(sync);
   S2;

Blocking semaphores



We add sleep() and wakeup() to the underlying implementation
Call to wait, and semaphore not available, process is blocked with sleep() (wait state) and placed on a waiting queue
Blocked processes are notified of an available semaphore by the wakeup() operation

goes from waiting to ready
Able to achieve bounded wait and progress with FIFO queue





wait(semaphore *S) {
   S-&gt;value--;
   if (S-&gt;value &lt; 0) {
      add process to S-&gt;list;
      sleep();
   }
}

signal(semaphore *S) {
   S-&gt;value++;
   if (S-&gt;value &lt;= 0) {
      remove P from S-&gt;list;
      wakeup(P);
   }
}

Deadlock example

Classic example: Thread1 holds lock L1 and is waiting for lock L2. Thread2 holds L2 and is waiting for L1
  Thread 1:                       Thread 2:
  pthread_mutex_lock(L1);         pthread_mutex_lock(L2);
  pthread_mutex_lock(L2);         pthread_mutex_lock(L1);

Attacking hold and wait

When a process requests a resource, it cannot be holding any other resources
A process must request all of its resources at one time
Example, treat group of lock acquisitions like a critical section and surround with a mutex
pthread_mutex_lock(global_lock);
pthread_mutex_lock(L1);
pthread_mutex_lock(L2);
...
pthread_mutex_unlock(global_lock);
See any downsides?

Must know all needed locks a priori
Will likely decrease concurrency
